-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What this means, recorded 2026-10-06.

The cause is the BASELINE, not the detectors and not the thresholds, which are
identical on both sides. `measure_normality` takes `quiet_run_ids` -- every
quiet run the store holds -- so the normality a run is scored against depends
on how large the store was when the pass ran. By the recorded policy the store
holds findings written under two of them:

    BASELINE.quiet_run_count = 60   on 378 runs
    BASELINE.quiet_run_count = 100  on  22 runs

`anom-016` named this hazard when it put the quiet runs on the record -- two
baselines under identical thresholds give different findings and are otherwise
indistinguishable -- and `eval-003`'s policy column is what made it visible
here. Neither prevents it, and nothing on the screen or in `eval-002`'s table
reports which baseline a figure was taken under.

It also inverts this item's earlier reading. The four bundles matching no claim
are NOT stale: run 71's bundle, written 2026-09-19, agrees with today's rebuild
to the second, and it is the stored FINDING that was produced under the other
baseline.

The two runs written before the stop (54 and 71) were restored from a dump
taken beforehand. The store is byte-for-byte as it was: 103 bundles, 8306
findings, 155 hypotheses, 20 runs at the 100-run baseline.

The fill is blocked on a decision that is not a code decision: re-score the
corpus under one baseline, which rewrites every detection figure of record, or
leave two in the store and report which is which.
